                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a53e7179-ed7d-34d1-a7ae-181d227e7974",
                        "entry_id": "8a26e8e3-2d3c-3ef7-af97-d9a67e37c03c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Nachgedanke, der einem erst dann kommt, wenn man bereits aufgebrochen ist (sich also zum Beispiel auf der Treppe befindet) und es zu spät ist"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "008cc0cb-0587-4fc1-84d7-dace37814a7a",
                                "sense_id": "a53e7179-ed7d-34d1-a7ae-181d227e7974",
                                "text": "Ich hätte in der Prüfung viel besser sein können, wenn mir die besten Ideen nicht erst als Treppenwitze gekommen wären."
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f950ae1b-944c-3191-a58c-d3db5626c858",
                        "entry_id": "8a26e8e3-2d3c-3ef7-af97-d9a67e37c03c",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Begebenheit, die in ihrer Wirkung an einen schlechten Scherz gemahnt"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "ironisch"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ac039ebf-c234-4b87-a64f-a197126e10c0",
                                "sense_id": "f950ae1b-944c-3191-a58c-d3db5626c858",
                                "text": "„Snowden habe Deutschland mit der Aufklärung des Skandals einen großen Dienst erwiesen, sagte er der Rheinischen Post. Dass er ausgerechnet in Moskau Hilfe suchen müsse, sei ein Treppenwitz.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"2\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "9aa76fd4-e2f2-40e1-827c-0a8453307a22",
                        "source_entry_id": "8a26e8e3-2d3c-3ef7-af97-d9a67e37c03c",
                        "source_sense_id": "a53e7179-ed7d-34d1-a7ae-181d227e7974",
                        "target_lang_word": "afterwit",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "afterwit",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a1bf835f-fa0f-3927-beec-5f8366fe51bb",
                                        "entry_id": "af0f037e-7a11-33b6-aa44-1086368a0e72",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Wisdom which comes after the event."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9b161862-1797-4946-8d9d-a9ad411d1983",
                                                "sense_id": "a1bf835f-fa0f-3927-beec-5f8366fe51bb",
                                                "text": "After wits are dearely bought, / Let thy fore-wit guide thy thought."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "56e4bb0a-268a-419e-ae4d-5611dfda0744",
                                                "sense_id": "a1bf835f-fa0f-3927-beec-5f8366fe51bb",
                                                "text": "1692, Roger L’Estrange (translator), Fables of Æsop, London: R. Sare et al., Fable 162, “A Nightingale and a Bat,”\nThere’s No Recalling of what’s Gone and Past; so that After-Wit comes too Late when the Mischief is Done."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e4870df5-18b3-404a-89f7-2e8345543f42",
                                                "sense_id": "a1bf835f-fa0f-3927-beec-5f8366fe51bb",
                                                "text": "Trust me, dear Yorick, this unwary pleasantry of thine will sooner or later bring thee into scrapes and difficulties, which no after-wit can extricate thee out of."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6b0cea5e-5110-4423-900b-831698445587",
                                                "sense_id": "a1bf835f-fa0f-3927-beec-5f8366fe51bb",
                                                "text": "There is always a danger of afterwit in estimating the early achievements of men who have achieved fame […]."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "56aa0b41-e6bf-35d8-91e0-0e14be579fef",
                                        "entry_id": "af0f037e-7a11-33b6-aa44-1086368a0e72",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The lack of forethought."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "cf26248d-70dc-33ea-8766-6b875ce35da6",
                                        "entry_id": "af0f037e-7a11-33b6-aa44-1086368a0e72",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A good comeback, retort one thinks of only after the end of discussion or after leaving a social gathering."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "6be4ce98-6bd1-4064-9837-e1f1e679e495",
                        "source_entry_id": "8a26e8e3-2d3c-3ef7-af97-d9a67e37c03c",
                        "source_sense_id": "a53e7179-ed7d-34d1-a7ae-181d227e7974",
                        "target_lang_word": "staircase wit",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "staircase wit",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f4ab3910-2b87-30da-9331-d67cbb2194b5",
                                        "entry_id": "816ec41f-7f58-39ef-b383-4c6cd0b1c87d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Thinking of an idea or course of action too late to use it effectively, or the tendency to do so."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "idiomatic",
                                            "rare",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bb6db6dd-fb49-418a-bd04-185ce767974f",
                                                "sense_id": "f4ab3910-2b87-30da-9331-d67cbb2194b5",
                                                "text": "When I have completed a manuscript, if I lay it away in a drawer or pigeon-hole, it lies there quietly enough, and my mind seems wholly discharged of it. But if I mail it, I straightaway find myself tormented with \"staircase wit\" in the shape of emendations."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "832e6e16-0bae-494d-beee-741ba31b531c",
                                                "sense_id": "f4ab3910-2b87-30da-9331-d67cbb2194b5",
                                                "text": "So-called Staircase Wit is another example, where we key ourselves up for an important event — an interview, say, or a date — rehearsing how we are going to present ourselves and what we are going to say. Then afterwards we lie awake thinking of all the witty, intelligent, charming things we should have done and said, and didn't because we were so busy trying to remember and revise the plan. . . ."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "de01beb5-1189-493f-be4b-a53020b09489",
                                                "sense_id": "f4ab3910-2b87-30da-9331-d67cbb2194b5",
                                                "text": "One of the most insightful thinkers I know, the computer entrepreneur Yossi Vardi, prompted me to summarize \"my idea\" while standing on one leg. It was not too convenient to stand one one leg after a few glasses of perfumed Riesling, so I failed in my improvisation. The next day I experienced staircase wit."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "abe363aa-0965-464b-a3f1-559eb1061d0c",
                        "source_entry_id": "8a26e8e3-2d3c-3ef7-af97-d9a67e37c03c",
                        "source_sense_id": "a53e7179-ed7d-34d1-a7ae-181d227e7974",
